.TH BABYSITD 8 "2026-11-15" "babysit" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
babysitd \- housekeeping service for babysit logs
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B babysitd
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B babysitd
looks after the log directory used by
.BR babysit (1).
It is meant to run as a systemd user service.
It does not poll: it sleeps for
.B check_interval
(6 hours by default), does the things below when it wakes, and goes
back to sleep.
.BR babysit (1)
wakes it early when there is something to pack; see
.BR "EARLY REQUESTS" .
.TP
.B Archive earlier boots
.B babysit
files its logs under
.IR LOGDIR / boot-time / ,
one directory per boot.
Every such directory other than the current boot's is packed, with
whatever generations and packed generations it holds, into
.PP
.RS
.RS
.IR LOGDIR /archive/babysit_logs_ boot-time .tar.gz
.RE
.RE
.IP
and then removed, so an archive is named after the boot its logs came from,
not after the time it was made.
The check is made on start and at every scheduled wake-up, so it does not
matter how long after a reboot the service first runs.
An existing archive is never overwritten; a second one for the same boot
gets a
.B .2
before the extension.
Archives are made with
.BR tar (1);
if that fails the directory is left in place and a warning is logged.
.TP
.B Pack superseded generations
Within a boot,
.B babysit
logs to
.IR boot-time / N / ,
and starts generation
.IR N +1
when a pid comes round again (see
.BR "PID REUSE" " in " babysit (1)).
Whenever it wakes, the service looks for generations of the current boot
that are not the highest.
Each one that has had no new log for two seconds and whose logs all lack a
writer is replaced by
.IR boot-time / N .tar.gz .
A generation with a command still logging to it is left alone; only while
one is waiting like this does the service look again every 30 seconds.
Whether a log has a writer is decided by the write lock its babysitter
holds, not by inspecting open file descriptors.
.TP
.B Pack early when the disk fills up
Whenever it wakes, the service checks how full the filesystem holding
.I LOGDIR
is.
At
.B compact_at_disk_percent
or more it starts a new generation itself, exactly as
.B babysit
would on a pid collision, so that the generation in use until then is packed
and compressed as soon as its commands have finished.
This is skipped while the current generation holds no logs, and done at most
once every ten minutes, so a disk that is full of other things does not
cause endless repacking.
Packing compresses logs; it does not delete any.
.TP
.B Delete under disk pressure
This is off unless
.B delete_at_disk_percent
is set.
When the filesystem is at least that full, the service deletes packed logs
one at a time, oldest first, until usage is back under the limit or none are
left.
The candidates are the archives of earlier boots and the packed generations
of the current boot; a live log directory is never deleted, so a command
that is still running, and a generation not yet packed, are safe.
Each deletion is logged as a warning.
If usage is still over the limit once every packed log is gone, the rest of
the space belongs to something else, and that is logged too.
.TP
.B Expire old logs
Archives and packed generations whose file is older than
.B max_age_days
are deleted, as are finished logs of the current boot whose newest file is
that old.
This is done on start and at every scheduled wake-up.
.TP
.B Watch for pid rollover
Whenever it wakes it reads the kernel's last allocated pid.
If the value has gone down since the last look, the pid range has wrapped
around and pids from this boot can now be reused; a warning is logged.
With hours between looks this is a best-effort notice and can miss a
wrap-around.
Nothing else is done:
.B babysit
handles an actual collision itself (see
.BR "PID REUSE" " in " babysit (1)).
.PP
A log directory that still has a writer is never archived or deleted.
Any doubt counts as a writer: a log the service cannot open, and a log on
a filesystem that cannot report locks (some network and FUSE filesystems),
is left where it is, and so is the generation or boot directory around it.
Such a directory is never packed or expired by the service.
.PP
The service only ever packs or deletes what
.BR babysit (1)
and it created: directories named like a boot
.RI ( YYYYmmdd-HHMMSS
or
.BR unknown-boot ),
the numbered generations and
.IB N .tar.gz
files inside the current one, and
.BI babysit_logs_ * .tar.gz
files in
.IR LOGDIR /archive .
Anything else found in
.I LOGDIR
is left alone.
It is still best to give
.B babysit
a directory of its own.
.PP
Only one
.B babysitd
works on a log directory at a time.
Each pass is made holding a lock on
.IR LOGDIR /.lock ,
so a second instance, such as a
.B \-\-once
run by hand or from cron while the service is running, waits for the pass in
progress to finish and then makes its own.
.PP
Messages go to stderr, which under systemd is the journal:
.PP
.RS
journalctl \-\-user \-u babysitd
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "\-l, \-\-log\-dir " DIR
Log directory to look after.
Default:
.IR $HOME/.local/var/log/babysit .
.TP
.BI "\-c, \-\-config " FILE
Configuration file; see
.BR babysit.conf (5).
Default:
.IR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/babysit/babysit.conf ,
or
.IR $HOME/.config/babysit/babysit.conf .
A missing default file is not an error.
.TP
.BI "\-a, \-\-max\-age\-days " N
Overrides
.B max_age_days
from the configuration file.
0 keeps logs forever.
Default: 30.
.TP
.BI "\-i, \-\-check\-interval " T
Overrides
.B check_interval
from the configuration file.
Default: 6h.
.TP
.BI "\-u, \-\-compact\-at\-disk\-percent " N
Overrides
.B compact_at_disk_percent
from the configuration file.
0 disables.
Default: 90.
.TP
.BI "\-D, \-\-delete\-at\-disk\-percent " N
Overrides
.B delete_at_disk_percent
from the configuration file.
0 disables.
Default: 0.
.TP
.BI "\-\-last\-pid\-file " FILE
File holding the last allocated pid.
Default:
.IR /proc/sys/kernel/ns_last_pid .
.TP
.B \-1, \-\-once
Archive and expire once, then exit.
Useful from a shutdown hook or by hand.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show a summary of options.
.SH EARLY REQUESTS
The service listens on the FIFO
.IR LOGDIR /.request .
When
.BR babysit (1)
starts a new log generation it writes one byte there, and the service wakes
at once and does its whole housekeeping pass, exactly as at a scheduled
wake-up.
That packs the generation that was just superseded (after the two-second
quiet period, and once its commands have finished).
.PP
The write never blocks and its failure is ignored, so
.B babysit
behaves the same whether or not the service is running.
If it is not, the superseded generation is simply packed the next time the
service starts.
Anything may wake the service the same way, which is also how to test it:
lay out a log directory by hand, then
.PP
.RS
echo 1 > $LOGDIR/.request
.RE
.PP
What is written does not matter.
Only do this while the service is running: a shell opening a FIFO that has
no reader waits until one appears.
.SH FAILURES
Packing needs room for the tarball before the directory it replaces can be
removed, so it can fail on a disk that is already full.
The service is written so that this, or being killed part-way, loses
nothing:
.IP \(bu 2
A tarball is written under a
.B .partial
name and renamed into place only when complete; the directory is removed
only after that.
.IP \(bu 2
If packing fails, the partial file is removed, the directory is left as it
was, a warning is logged, and packing is not attempted again for ten
minutes.
.IP \(bu 2
A packed directory is removed by first renaming it to
.IB name .removing ,
which either happens completely or not at all.
A run killed while removing it therefore never leaves a half-empty
directory under its real name, which the next run would pack again over the
complete tarball.
.IP \(bu 2
.B .partial
files and
.B .removing
directories left by a run that was killed are removed on the next start.
.IP \(bu 2
If a run was killed after the tarball was renamed into place but before the
directory was renamed away, the directory is still whole, and the next run
packs it again and replaces the tarball with an identical one.
.PP
.BR babysit (1)
never waits on any of this.
A command whose log cannot be created because the disk is full is not
started, and
.B babysit
reports the error.
.SH INSTALLATION
.RS
.nf
systemctl \-\-user daemon\-reload
systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now babysitd
.fi
.RE
.PP
A user service starts when the user's systemd instance starts, which is at
first login unless lingering is enabled
.RB ( "loginctl enable\-linger" ).
Because each boot logs to its own directory, a late start loses nothing:
the earlier boot's logs are still there, untouched, to be archived.
.SH WITHOUT SYSTEMD
.B babysitd
does not depend on systemd; the unit file is only a convenient way to start
it.
It is an ordinary foreground program that any supervisor can run as the user
whose logs it looks after.
On FreeBSD, other BSDs and macOS there are two ways to run it from that
user's
.BR crontab (1).
.PP
As a long-running process started at boot, which keeps early requests
working:
.PP
.RS
@reboot /usr/local/bin/babysitd
.RE
.PP
Or with no long-running process at all, as a periodic one-shot:
.PP
.RS
0 */6 * * * /usr/local/bin/babysitd \-\-once
.RE
.PP
In the second form nothing listens on the request FIFO, so a superseded
generation is packed at the next run instead of at once.
Nothing is lost either way:
.BR babysit (1)
never depends on the service.
.SH PORTABILITY
Boots are told apart by the kernel's boot id on Linux and by the boot time
(from
.BR sysctl (3))
on the BSDs and macOS.
On a system that offers neither,
.B babysit
logs everything under
.IR LOGDIR /unknown-boot ,
which is always treated as the current boot and so is never archived.
.PP
The last allocated pid is read from
.I /proc/sys/kernel/ns_last_pid
on Linux and from
.B kern.lastpid
on FreeBSD; elsewhere the rollover watch is switched off with a notice.
.PP
Linux and FreeBSD are the supported systems.
macOS and the other BSDs are best effort and are not tested.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR babysit (1),
.BR babysit.conf (5)
